course: dictionary
lesson: 15468
title: Storehouse: a place full of things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A storehouse is a building where food, goods or supplies are kept until they are needed. Store means keep for later, and house means building, so the word puts those two ideas together. «The farmer filled the storehouse with sacks of wheat before winter.»
explain: People also use storehouse to describe a place or person that has a huge amount of something. «My grandad is a storehouse of stories.» Grandad is not a building full of food. The clue is that he knows an enormous number of stories, so storehouse means full of something good.
explain: In Bible stories, storehouses kept grain safe so people had bread in hard times. When you meet storehouse in a text, ask: is it a real building, or is it a picture of something full of a lot of things? The words around it will tell you which.
fact: A storehouse is a building where food, goods or supplies are kept.
fact: «A storehouse of knowledge» means a person or place with a great deal of knowledge.
fact: A grain storehouse keeps wheat and other grain safe for later use.
fact: In the Bible story of Joseph in Egypt, storehouses held grain for hard times.
fact: The word storehouse joins store (keep for later) and house (a building).
exercise: «The farmer filled the storehouse with sacks of wheat.» What is a storehouse? | A hole dug in the ground for a dog | A shop where you pay at the till | A small boat used for fishing | *A building where food and supplies are kept
exercise: «My grandad is a storehouse of stories.» What does storehouse mean here? | Empty and quiet | Closed for the winter | Made of stone and wood | *Full of a lot of stories
exercise: In a grain storehouse, what is kept safe? | Toys and games | Fish and water | *Grain, such as wheat | Books and paper
test: Why were storehouses important in the Bible story of Joseph? | To store songs and poems | *To keep grain for hard times | A place to keep boats dry | A place to keep toys for the children
test: «The museum is a storehouse of ancient coins.» What does this tell you? | The museum sells coins in its shop | The coins are very small and light | *The museum holds a great many ancient coins | The museum is closed for repairs
exam: Which sentence uses storehouse to mean a place full of something? | The storehouse by the river was locked | The storehouse was full of tins of beans | The storehouse was built with red bricks | *Her head is a storehouse of facts about space
